import Foundation

/// Independently written teaching text for the traditional half-square frog.
/// The diagram publisher's 22 groups are mapped in docs/FROG_SOURCES.md.
enum FrogLessonContent {
    static let material = "One 7.5 × 15 cm rectangle · No cutting or glue during folding"
    static let sourceName = "Origami.me · Traditional Jumping Frog"
    static let sourceURL = URL(string: "https://origami.me/jumping-frog/")!
    static let steps: [LessonStep] = {
        let rows: [(String,String,String,String)] = [
            ("Prepare a long rectangle", "Use a rectangle twice as long as it is wide. Place a short edge toward you, with the side you want outside facing down.", "7.5 × 15 CM · SHORT EDGE NEAR YOU", "This is the half-square version of the traditional jumping frog. Medium-weight paper gives the spring more resistance than very thin paper."),
            ("Mark the halfway line", "Bring the far short edge to the near short edge. Press the fold, then open the rectangle again.", "SHORT EDGES TOGETHER · REOPEN", "The center line separates the head end from the body end. Keep the paper in this orientation throughout the early folds."),
            ("Crease the first head diagonal", "On the far half, bring the far-right corner diagonally across. Match the short edge to the middle line, press, and open.", "FAR-RIGHT CORNER ACROSS · OPEN", "Only the far square needs this diagonal. The long body section stays flat while the corner travels across it."),
            ("Crease the second diagonal", "Repeat with the far-left corner. Press the diagonal and open the paper so the two lines cross.", "MAKE AN X IN THE FAR HALF", "These diagonal hinges let the head collapse into a triangle while all the paper stays connected."),
            ("Make the backward head crease", "Fold the far edge backward to the center line, press, then open again. Lift the sheet to give the paper room underneath.", "FAR EDGE BACKWARD · THEN OPEN", "This crease bends in the opposite direction from the diagonals. You can turn the paper over to make it, then turn it back."),
            ("Collapse the triangular head", "Push the two sides of the far square toward each other. Let its short edge move toward the center line and flatten the triangle.", "SIDES IN · TRIANGLE FLAT", "This waterbomb collapse uses three crease directions together. The animation shows the side panels closing inside rather than passing through the paper."),
            ("Form the first front leg", "Fold the top left triangular flap toward the head. Follow the slanted guide from the middle and let the point reach upward and slightly outward.", "LEFT TOP FLAP ONLY", "Move the loose flap and its folded-under layer together. The broad triangular layer underneath must stay in place."),
            ("Match the other front leg", "Fold the top right flap upward to match. Compare the two points before pressing the second crease.", "MATCH THE TWO FRONT LEGS", "These folds have no single exact angle. Symmetry matters more than copying a measurement precisely."),
            ("Fold up the body end", "Bring the near short edge to the line where the triangular head begins. Press the horizontal crease and leave this section folded.", "NEAR EDGE TO THE HEAD BASE", "This doubles the body section. Keep the front legs free instead of trapping them inside the fold."),
            ("Narrow the left side", "Lift the left front leg slightly. Fold the long left body edge to the center, including the triangular layer behind the leg.", "LEFT BODY EDGE TO CENTER", "The loose front leg stays outside this fold. Its attachment is near the middle, so the side beneath it can fold independently."),
            ("Narrow the right side", "Repeat with the right body edge. Both long edges should meet along the middle.", "TWO BODY EDGES MEET", "A straight center join keeps the back legs and spring balanced. Do not fold the front leg into the body."),
            ("Fold the rear section up", "Fold the near edge upward to the point where the front legs meet. Keep the rear flap on top.", "NEAR EDGE TO THE LEG JUNCTION", "This creates the horizontal hinge that will later open into the wide back-leg pocket."),
            ("Mark the left rear diagonal", "Fold the top flap's left upper corner down toward the near edge along the guide. Press, then open just this diagonal fold.", "LEFT REAR CORNER DOWN · OPEN", "The diagonal runs from the middle of the top edge toward the lower-left corner. It will guide the pocket outward."),
            ("Mark the right rear diagonal", "Repeat the same crease on the right. Open the diagonal again and leave the rectangular flap flat.", "MATCH THE RIGHT REAR DIAGONAL", "The two temporary folds form matching guides. Neither corner should stay tucked in at the end of this action."),
            ("Open the rear section", "Undo the large upward fold. Leave the two long side folds in place and expose the diagonal guides.", "OPEN ONLY THE LAST LARGE FLAP", "The paper is now ready to spread sideways. Keeping the long edges folded preserves the layers needed for the hind legs."),
            ("Spread the rear pocket", "Lift the near edge toward the leg junction. Ease the two loose corners outward along the diagonal guides, then flatten the wide pocket.", "CORNERS OUT · EDGE UP", "This is a linked squash fold. The two corners travel sideways while the middle folds upward; do not pull the layers apart."),
            ("Lower the left hind flap", "Fold the left outward-pointing flap down along the existing diagonal. Its long point should face toward you.", "LEFT SIDE POINT DOWN", "Only the loose flap moves. The short rectangular section behind it stays flat and will support the spring."),
            ("Lower the right hind flap", "Bring the matching right flap down. The two long points now meet near the middle.", "MATCH THE TWO LOWER POINTS", "Check the two flaps before making their foot folds. They should have matching lengths and edges."),
            ("Fold the left hind foot outward", "Fold the inner edge of the left flap toward its diagonal outer crease. Let the long point swing outward.", "LEFT INNER EDGE TO DIAGONAL", "This narrows the flap and separates the foot from the body's center. Follow the highlighted top layers only."),
            ("Fold the right hind foot outward", "Repeat on the right and compare the two broad feet. Keep their tips spread apart.", "TWO HIND FEET POINT OUTWARD", "The back feet provide a broad support for the spring. Symmetric folds help the real frog push off evenly."),
            ("Fold the spring forward", "Fold the entire rear section toward the head along the line where the front and back legs meet.", "REAR SECTION FORWARD", "This is the first half of the spring. The back feet travel with the body section, rather than folding separately."),
            ("Fold the spring back", "Fold the top rear flap halfway back toward you. Press the short horizontal fold firmly to make a zigzag pleat.", "TOP FLAP HALFWAY BACK", "The two opposing folds work together as a paper spring. This hinge needs a firm crease, especially with thicker paper."),
            ("Turn over and release the legs", "Turn the frog over. Gently ease the front legs and spring open so the body stands above its feet. On your real frog, press the back lightly and release.", "FOUR LEGS · SPRING UNDER THE BODY", "The animation releases existing folds; it adds no new creases. Your real frog's jump depends on the paper and crease tension. The model shows the structure, not a prediction of jump height.")
        ]
        return rows.enumerated().map { i,row in LessonStep(id:i,title:row.0,instruction:row.1,cue:row.2,explanation:row.3) }
    }()
}
